# RedMed — project rules for AI coding assistants

**Product is the NFC band**, not the app. The passive bracelet holds emergency
medical ID on an NTAG chip. **Tap the band → any smartphone opens the emergency
card in the browser.** No RedMed install for readers. The iPhone app is a
one-time setup tool (CoreNFC write) — band-first, app-second in all copy.

Local-first. No backend, no accounts, nothing sent to a server by design —
keep it that way unless explicitly told to change it.

**Product surface:** native **iOS** (`ios/RedMed/` SwiftUI) only. No Android,
no owner web UI, no desktop owner path. `mac/` is dev Simulator helper only.

**Passive NFC only:** NTAG-style ISO 14443 tags — no BLE, no active RFID, no
battery-powered bands. Do not add radio features beyond phone-tap NDEF URI records.

## Sole repository

This product lives in **one** GitHub repo only:
`https://github.com/Roooted1776/RedMed`.

Do not create, recommend, or depend on a second repo. Support is email only
(`help.RedMed@gmail.com`). Pages host `card/` (bracelet taps), legal pages,
and `get.html` (owner band setup QR).

## Where things live

- `ios/RedMed/` — **setup app** (SwiftUI). CoreNFC write/read, native scanned card.
- `card/` — **the band's output** — hosted emergency card (`#d=`) on smartphone tap.
- `get.html` — packaging QR → App Store (program band once on iPhone).
- `mac/RedMed.app/` — **dev only** — Simulator launcher, not a product surface.

## Things that will silently break if you miss them

- **Band-first messaging.** Responders need only any smartphone — never imply they need the app.
- **HTTPS on the chip.** New tags store hosted `card/#d=…`. Passersby tap → browser card.
- **Passive NFC only.** No BLE, no continuous broadcast.
- After `get.html` / `card/` edits: recompute CSP hash; `./scripts/sync.sh www`.

## Conventions

- Comments explain *why*, not *what*.
- Swift edits: brace/paren balance check before commit.
